    "summary": "Houthi officials claim Saudi-led coalition airstrikes hit a commercial area near Taiz's central market on Sunday, killing at least seven and wounding dozens. The coalition has not commented.",
    "content": "On Sunday, Houthi authorities in Yemen accused the Saudi-led coalition of conducting an airstrike on a commercial area near the central market in Taiz, a city in southwestern Yemen. According to Houthi statements, the attack resulted in the deaths of at least seven people and injuries to dozens more. The reported strike targeted a location where civilians were present, though the exact circumstances remain unclear.\n\nTaiz has been a focal point of the conflict between Houthi forces and the Saudi-backed Yemeni government since 2015. The city, Yemen's third-largest, has seen frequent clashes and airstrikes, with both sides accusing each other of targeting civilians. The Saudi-led coalition, which intervened in Yemen in 2015 to restore the internationally recognized government, has conducted numerous airstrikes across the country, often citing military targets. Houthi-controlled areas have regularly reported civilian casualties from such strikes.\n\nThe Houthi-run health ministry in Taiz reported the casualties, but independent verification is challenging due to restricted access and ongoing hostilities. The Saudi-led coalition has not yet responded to these specific allegations. Previous incidents have seen the coalition acknowledge some strikes while denying others, often stating that investigations are underway.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the conflict in Yemen is of particular concern due to historical and familial ties. Yemen has long been a transit point for Somali refugees and migrants seeking to reach Gulf countries, and many Somali families have relatives living in Yemen. Additionally, the Saudi-led coalition includes troops from Somalia's neighbors, and the broader instability in Yemen affects regional trade and security, which can impact remittances and travel routes for Somalis.\n\nThe international community has repeatedly called for restraint and accountability for civilian casualties in Yemen. The United Nations has documented numerous incidents where airstrikes have hit civilian infrastructure, but holding parties accountable has proven difficult. This latest allegation adds to the ongoing concern over the protection of civilians in Yemen's protracted conflict.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/yemen-xuutiyiinta-oo-sheegay-in-duqeymo-sacuudiga-lagu-dilay-dad-ku-sugnaa-suuqa-taiz/425982/",
