    "summary": "Both the South West administration and supporters of former leader Abdiaziz Laftagareen claim victories after fighting in Baidoa, as tensions persist over regional leadership.",
    "content": "Clashes erupted in Baidoa, the interim capital of Somalia's South West state, between forces loyal to the current regional administration and armed supporters of former South West President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen. Both sides have issued statements claiming to have inflicted losses on the other and to have made territorial gains. The fighting marks a serious escalation in the long-running dispute over the regional presidency.\n\nThe political crisis in South West state stems from last year's disputed election that extended Laftagareen's term, which the federal government and opposition groups rejected. In response, the federal authorities recognized a new administration led by Abdikadir Sharif Shekhuna Maye, prompting Laftagareen to withdraw to areas around Baidoa and refuse to step down. Since then, intermittent violence has flared as both sides jockey for control.\n\nThe latest confrontations reportedly involved heavy weaponry and caused civilian displacement, though exact casualty figures remain unverified. Local sources indicate that businesses in parts of Baidoa have closed and that some residents are fleeing the city. The South West administration has accused Laftagareen's militia of attacking government positions, while Laftagareen's faction says it acted in self-defense against an offensive by federal-backed forces.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the instability in Baidoa is deeply concerning. Many diaspora members have family ties to the South West region and remit funds that sustain local livelihoods. The violence threatens to disrupt humanitarian access in an area already facing drought and food insecurity. Additionally, the political impasse undermines ongoing efforts to stabilize Somalia ahead of planned national elections, a process in which diaspora voices and investments are heavily vested.\n\nInternational partners, including the United Nations and neighboring countries, have called for an immediate ceasefire and dialogue. The federal government has not issued a detailed statement on the latest fighting, but previous attempts at mediation have failed to resolve the leadership dispute. As the situation remains fluid, the risk of further clashes persists, with potential ripple effects across the region.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/dhinacyadii-ku-dagaalamay-magaalada-baydhabo-oo-guulo-sheegtay/425972/",
