    "original_title": "IGAD oo Muqdisho tababar ku saabsan nabad-dhisidda iyo hoggaaminta u qabatay haween Soomaaliyeed",
    "slug": "igad-opens-leadership-and-peacebuilding-training-for-somali-women-in-mogadishu",
    "summary": "IGAD begins a three-day training in Mogadishu for nearly 20 Somali women on peacebuilding and leadership, in partnership with Somalia's Ministry of Family and Human Rights.",
    "content": "The Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) launched a three-day training program in Mogadishu on September 28, 2026, aimed at strengthening the role of Somali women in peacebuilding, conflict resolution, and participation in leadership and decision-making processes. The program is being implemented in collaboration with Somalia's Ministry of Family and Human Rights Development.\n\nApproximately 20 women are participating in the training, including female leaders and members of local peace committees. The curriculum covers skills such as conflict analysis, mediation, and leadership, according to organizers. The initiative is part of broader efforts by IGAD to enhance women's inclusion in peace and security efforts in the region.\n\nSomalia has faced decades of conflict, and women have often been excluded from formal peace processes despite their significant roles in community reconciliation and support for families. In recent years, international and regional organizations have increased efforts to integrate women into peace and state-building initiatives, aligning with United Nations Security Council Resolution 1325 on Women, Peace, and Security.\n\nThe training comes amid ongoing efforts by the Somali federal government and regional partners to stabilize the country and promote inclusive governance. Women's participation in peacebuilding is seen as crucial for sustainable peace, as they can bridge community divides and advocate for issues often overlooked in male-dominated forums.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development highlights the importance of supporting women's leadership in Somalia. Many diaspora women are actively involved in peacebuilding and development initiatives, and such trainings can complement their efforts by building local capacity. The program also underscores the role of regional bodies like IGAD in fostering gender-inclusive peace processes that can benefit the entire region.\n\nThe three-day training is expected to conclude with participants developing action plans to apply their skills in their communities. IGAD and the Somali government have not yet announced plans for follow-up activities, but similar programs have been held in other member states. The initiative reflects a growing recognition of women's contributions to peace and security in the Horn of Africa.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/28/igad-oo-muqdisho-tababar-ku-saabsan-nabad-dhisidda-iyo-hoggaaminta-u-qabatay-haween-soomaaliyeed/",
